# Since .env is gitignored, you can use .env.example to build a new `.env` file when you clone the repo.
# Keep this file up-to-date when you add new variables to `.env`.
#
# This file is committed to version control, so never put real secrets in it.
# If you are cloning this repo, copy it to `.env` and fill in your own values:
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# Env schemas live in apps/nextjs/src/env.ts, packages/auth/env.ts and
# packages/email/src/env.ts. They validate at startup and fail loudly when a
# required variable is missing (validation is skipped when CI=1,
# SKIP_ENV_VALIDATION=1 or during lint; the Dockerfile sets SKIP_ENV_VALIDATION
# because no real value exists while the image is built).
#
# This file only matters for local development. In production nothing is read
# from a file: dokku injects every variable into the container (`dokku
# config:set`, plus POSTGRES_URL from `dokku postgres:link`), see README.md.

# --- app (required) -----------------------------------------------------------
# Canonical public origin of the web app, no trailing slash. `metadataBase`
# (absolute og image / canonical urls) and the sitemap are built from it. The
# email templates (packages/email), the meetup ical url (packages/api) and
# openGraph.url still hard-code https://www.laundryroom.social, so a staging
# box with a different APP_URL keeps sending production links from those.
# Production: https://www.laundryroom.social
# 127.0.0.1, not localhost: atproto's dev (loopback) client only calls back there
APP_URL='http://127.0.0.1:3000'

# --- database (required) ------------------------------------------------------
# Any Postgres connection string, read by packages/db/src/client.ts and by
# drizzle-kit (packages/db/drizzle.config.ts, `pnpm db:push`). A hosted dev
# database such as Neon needs `?sslmode=verify-full` (node-postgres 8 treats
# `require` as an alias for it but prints a security warning on every start, and
# pg 9 will turn `require` into an unverified connection); the dokku postgres
# plugin in production talks plain tcp on the docker network and injects
# something like postgres://postgres:<pw>@dokku-postgres-laundryroom-db:5432/laundryroom_db
POSTGRES_URL='postgres://user:password@host:5432/dbname?sslmode=verify-full'

# --- auth (required) ----------------------------------------------------------
# Better Auth (packages/auth/src/index.ts): google oauth + email magic links.
#
# Secret that signs the session cookie and the 5-minute cookie cache. Optional
# in development (Better Auth falls back to a built-in dev secret and warns),
# required in production, where it refuses to start without one. Rotating it
# signs everyone out. Generate one with: openssl rand -base64 32
# @see https://www.better-auth.com/docs/installation#set-environment-variables
AUTH_SECRET='supersecret'

# Google OAuth, the main login provider. The authorized redirect uri in the
# google console is unchanged from the Auth.js days:
#   https://www.laundryroom.social/api/auth/callback/google
# @see https://www.better-auth.com/docs/authentication/google
AUTH_GOOGLE_ID=''
AUTH_GOOGLE_SECRET=''

# Public origin Better Auth builds its absolute urls from (the google callback,
# the sign-in link in the magic-link email) and trusts for the origin check on
# sign-in requests. Required in production: the standalone server behind the
# dokku nginx proxy only knows its listen address (0.0.0.0:3000). Locally it
# falls back to APP_URL and, when that is unset too, to the origin of each
# request (`next dev` on localhost). Set it to your lan address (or leave both
# unset) when testing the expo app against a dev server on another device.
# AUTH_URL='https://www.laundryroom.social'

# --- atproto sign-in (optional) -----------------------------------------------
# Sign in with an atproto (bluesky) account, packages/auth/src/atproto. Google
# and magic links keep working next to it.
#
# Development needs nothing here: with a loopback APP_URL (localhost or
# 127.0.0.1) the keyless atproto loopback client is used. Its callback has to
# be http://127.0.0.1:<port> (the authorization server never redirects to
# "localhost"), and cookies are per host, so in development every page opened
# on localhost or [::1] redirects to 127.0.0.1 (apps/nextjs/src/middleware.ts)
# and you sign in there.
#
# Production: an ES256 private key as a JWK (JSON on one line, kid "k1"), which
# turns on the confidential client for an https APP_URL. The client id is
# ${APP_URL}/oauth-client-metadata.json and the public key is published at
# ${APP_URL}/oauth/jwks.json. Every oauth session is bound to the key that
# created it: replacing the key signs everyone out of their atproto session,
# so a rotation adds a second key (k2, see packages/auth/src/atproto/keys.ts)
# and keeps k1 until its sessions have expired. Generate one with
#
#   node -e "const {generateKeyPairSync}=require('node:crypto');const {privateKey}=generateKeyPairSync('ec',{namedCurve:'P-256'});console.log(JSON.stringify({...privateKey.export({format:'jwk'}),kid:'k1',alg:'ES256',key_ops:['sign']}))"
#
# (key_ops ["sign"] is the signing-only "use": "sig" of older guides;
# @atproto/jwk-jose deprecates "use" on private keys) and set it with
# dokku config:set laundryroom ATPROTO_OAUTH_PRIVATE_JWK='<the json>'.
# ATPROTO_OAUTH_PRIVATE_JWK=''
#
# PDS that hosts "create an account" (prompt=create), e.g. our own once it
# exists. Unset: the login page shows no sign-up button.
# ATPROTO_SIGNUP_PDS_URL='https://pds.lndry.me'
#
# Development only (ignored in production and for a non-loopback APP_URL):
# sign in against a local network such as @atproto/dev-env instead of the real
# one: the plc directory, a pds or appview that answers
# com.atproto.identity.resolveHandle, and the appview for display names.
# Setting any of them also allows plain-http pdses and turns off the ssrf
# filter of the oauth client.
# ATPROTO_DEV_PLC_URL='http://localhost:2582'
# ATPROTO_DEV_HANDLE_RESOLVER='http://localhost:2583'
# ATPROTO_DEV_PUBLIC_APPVIEW='http://localhost:2584'

# --- email (required) ---------------------------------------------------------
# Resend api key. Used for the magic-link sign-in email (packages/auth via
# packages/email) and for transactional emails like meetup invitations.
# @see https://resend.com/api-keys
RESEND_KEY=''

# --- llm (required in production, optional locally) ---------------------------
# OpenAI credentials for packages/llm (content moderation and search text
# generation). The client is created lazily on the first llm call (creating a
# group, comment or discussion), so without a key only those calls fail.
# Org and project ids are optional and only matter if your key belongs to several.
# OPENAI_API_KEY=''
# OPENAI_ORG_ID=''
# OPENAI_PROJECT_ID=''

# --- file uploads (required for image uploads) --------------------------------
# Vercel Blob read/write token, read implicitly by @vercel/blob in
# apps/nextjs/src/app/api/upload/route.ts. Uploads stay on Vercel Blob after
# the move to dokku; the token is all it needs. Without it, group and profile
# image uploads fail; everything else keeps working.
# @see https://vercel.com/docs/storage/vercel-blob
BLOB_READ_WRITE_TOKEN=''

# --- misc (optional) ----------------------------------------------------------
# Port the server listens on. The dev server and the server-side tRPC client
# (apps/nextjs/src/trpc/react.tsx, which calls itself on localhost) both read
# it. Defaults to 3000. The Dockerfile sets it to 3000 as well and dokku maps
# 80/443 onto that port, so do not `config:set PORT` on the dokku app.
# PORT=3000
#
# Set by the Dockerfile for the image build only, never at runtime. It only
# skips the env schemas above; the placeholder secret packages/auth uses while
# `next build` evaluates the route modules is keyed to next's own build phase
# (NEXT_PHASE), so setting this on the dokku app does not weaken anything, the
# server refuses to start without AUTH_SECRET in production either way.
# SKIP_ENV_VALIDATION=1
